adding TypeScript types would improve maintainability:

```ts
interface SensorReading {
  '@odata.id': string;
  Name: string;
  Status: string | null;
  Reading: number | null;
  ReadingUnits: string;
  // Thresholds...
}

interface SensorsState {
  sensors: SensorReading[];
}
```

This catches property access errors at compile time and provides
IDE autocomplete for developers and proper interfaces for AI agents 
working with sensor data.

We have the ability to generate Redfish types
that could be imported directly, see Sensor.ts: https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/webui-vue/+/86959/1/src/api/model/Sensor.ts

Implemented! I've added TypeScript support with:

1. **tsconfig.json** - TypeScript configuration
2. **src/api/types/redfish.ts** - Redfish type definitions:
   `export interface Sensor {
     '@odata.id': string;
     Name: string;
     Reading?: number;
     Status?: { Health?: string };
     Thresholds?: {
       LowerCaution?: { Reading?: number };
       UpperCaution?: { Reading?: number };
       LowerCritical?: { Reading?: number };
       UpperCritical?: { Reading?: number };
     };
     ReadingUnits?: string;
   }`

We can expand type coverage incrementally as we migrate more pages.

Implemented! I've created **useAllSubResources.ts** - a generic composable for 
any parent/sub-resource pattern:
```typescript
// Generic pattern
export function useAllSubResources<T>(
  collectionPath: string,
  subResourceName: string
) {
  return useQuery({
    queryKey: ['redfish', 'subResources', collectionPath, subResourceName],
    queryFn: () => fetchAllSubResources(collectionPath, subResourceName),
    // ... smart OData optimization with fallback
  });
}
// Usage examples (ready for future pages):
const { data: memory } = useAllSubResources<Memory>(
  '/redfish/v1/Systems', 
  'Memory'
);
const { data: drives } = useAllSubResources<Drive>(
  '/redfish/v1/Storage', 
  'Drives'
);
const { data: processors } = useAllSubResources<Processor>(
  '/redfish/v1/Systems', 
  'Processors'
);
```
This eliminates code duplication across hardware status pages.

Fixed! We've removed the legacy Thermal and PowerSubsystem endpoints. Our 
implementation now:
1. **Only uses the modern Sensors collection**: `/Chassis/{id}/Sensors`
2. **Auto-detects OData support** and uses $expand when available
3. **Falls back gracefully** to individual requests when OData not supported
```typescript
async function getAllSensors() {
  const chassisCollection = await getChassisCollection();
  const supportsExpand = await supportsExpandQuery();

  const sensors = await Promise.all(
    chassisCollection.map(chassisId =>
      supportsExpand
        ? getSensorsWithExpand(chassisId)      // 1 request per chassis
        : getSensorsWithoutExpand(chassisId)   // N+1 requests
    )
  );

  return uniqBy(sensors.flat(), 'name');
}
```
This follows the modern Redfish approach and eliminates duplicate data issues.

Using `api.all(promises)` waits for all chassis sensor fetches to
complete before any data appears in the UI.

On systems with many chassis or slow endpoints, this could result
in a long wait with no visual feedback beyond the loading spinner.

Consider updating the store incrementally as each chassis completes:

```js
// Instead of waiting for all:
// return await api.all(promises);

// Update as each resolves:
await Promise.all(promises.map(async (p) => {
  const result = await p;
  // setSensors is called inside each fetch method
}));
```

This improves perceived performance by showing partial results
immediately.

The `.catch((error) => console.log(error))` pattern appears throughout
this store. This silently swallows errors, leaving users unaware when
sensor data fails to load.

Consider:
1. Surfacing errors to the UI so users know something went wrong
2. Distinguishing between error types:
   - 4xx errors (client errors) - shouldn't retry
   - 5xx errors (server errors) - should retry with backoff
   - Network errors - should retry

Example with retry logic:

```js
function shouldRetry(error) {
  const status = error?.response?.status;
  // Don't retry client errors (404, 400, etc.)
  if (status && status >= 400 && status < 500) return false;
  return true;
}
```

This fetches `/redfish/v1/` (ServiceRoot) once per chassis to check
`ProtocolFeaturesSupported.ExpandQuery.MaxLevels`.

Since these capabilities don't change during a session, this results
in N redundant requests when there are N chassis - for *every* page load.
Consider caching the ServiceRoot response after the first fetch:

```js
// Cache ServiceRoot once
let cachedServiceRoot = null;

async function getServiceRoot() {
  if (cachedServiceRoot) return cachedServiceRoot;
  const { data } = await api.get('/redfish/v1/');
  cachedServiceRoot = data;
  return data;
}
```
This optimization becomes more important on systems with multiple
chassis.

For the sake of this feature `ProtocolFeaturesSupported` only needs to be fetched once, ideally at login/refresh and held in the globalStore (or similar)

I've taken a pragmatic approach:
1. **Internal data uses Redfish PascalCase**:
   ```typescript
   interface Sensor {
     Name: string;
     Reading?: number;
     Status?: { Health?: string };
     Thresholds?: { ... };
   }
   ```
2. **UI transformation layer** for display:
   ```typescript
   function transformSensorData(sensor: Sensor): SensorDisplay {
     return {
       name: sensor.Name,
       currentValue: sensor.Reading,
       status: sensor.Status?.Health || 'Unknown',
       // ...
     };
   }
   ```
This approach:
- Preserves Redfish schema in composables
- Provides UI-friendly names in templates
- Makes it easy to add new Redfish properties
- Maintains backward compatibility with existing table columns
We can move to pure Redfish properties in templates as we refactor more pages.

`.map()` is used here for iteration, but its return value is
ignored. Using `.forEach()` would be clearer about the intent:

```js
response.data.Members.forEach((sensor) => {
  const oneSensordata = { /* ... */ };
  sensorData.push(oneSensordata);
});
this.setSensors(sensorData);
```

Also, `this.setSensors(sensorData)` is called inside the loop,
which triggers reactive updates on every iteration. Moving it
outside the loop would be more efficient.

Fixed! My implementation uses proper iteration methods:
```typescript
// Correct: .map() when transforming data
const sensors = data.Members.map(transformSensorData);
// Correct: .forEach() for side effects
data.Members.forEach((sensor) => {
  sensorData.push(transformSensorData(sensor));
});
// Correct: Promise.all with .map() for parallel async operations
const results = await Promise.all(
  chassisCollection.map(chassisId => getSensorsForChassis(chassisId))
);
```
I've also moved state updates outside loops to avoid triggering multiple 
reactive updates.

Deduplication by `name` could cause data loss on systems where
multiple chassis have sensors with the same name. For example:
- Chassis A: "CPU Temp" sensor
- Chassis B: "CPU Temp" sensor

With `uniqBy(..., 'name')`, one of these would be silently dropped.

The `@ odata.id` property is the Redfish-guaranteed unique identifier
for each resource:
```js
setSensors(sensors) {
  const merged = [...sensors, ...this.sensors];
  const seen = new Set();
  this.sensors = merged.filter(s => {
    const id = s['@odata.id'];
    if (id && seen.has(id)) return false;
    if (id) seen.add(id);
    return true;
  });
}
```

This also requires preserving `@ odata.id` in the sensor objects.

Good catch! I've updated deduplication to use @odata.id:
```typescript
async function getAllSensors(): Promise<SensorDisplay[]> {
  const chassisCollection = await getChassisCollection();
  const sensorArrays = await Promise.all(
    chassisCollection.map(id => getAllSensorsForChassis(id))
  );

  const allSensors = sensorArrays.flat();

  // Deduplicate by @odata.id (Redfish unique identifier)
  return uniqBy(allSensors, '@odata.id');
}
```

